# AI Grounding Contract

Every fact the user reads must be traceable to a `knowledge/services/*.yaml`
record. The server enforces this. Never rely on prompt wording alone — a system
prompt asking the model not to invent things is a request, not a guarantee.

## Never invent

Not government procedures, offices, fees, forms, documents, URLs, regulations,
processing times, or legal requirements. When the knowledge base does not cover
something, say it is not covered and point to the official source. "I don't know"
is a correct answer. A plausible guess is a defect.

## Model roles come from config, never hardcoded

```ts
// BAD
const model = openrouter.chat('some-vendor/some-model');

// GOOD — env-driven, swappable per environment
const model = openrouter.chat(env.OPENROUTER_ROUTER_MODEL);
```

Three roles: `OPENROUTER_ROUTER_MODEL` (classify and route), `OPENROUTER_ANSWER_MODEL`
(write the explanation), `OPENROUTER_SUMMARY_MODEL` (background rollups). Never
write a model ID from memory — model IDs are released and retired constantly.

## Route into a closed set

The router must pick from an enum built at runtime from the actual catalog slugs,
using `Output.choice({ options })` or an equivalent constrained output. The model
must be structurally incapable of naming a service that does not exist. Never ask
it to free-write a slug and then look that slug up.

If the router returns no match, respond "not covered" **without calling the answer
model at all**. Do not spend a second model call inventing an apology.

## Facts are rendered, never generated

Documents, fees, offices, and processing times are read out of the YAML record by
React components. The answer model receives only the one matched record and only
writes the connective prose around those fields. It never emits a fee.

## Validate citations server-side

Ask for `{ explanation, citations: string[] }` and verify every id against the
record before the response leaves the server:

```ts
const { known, unknown } = partitionCitations(service, output.citations);
if (unknown.length > 0 || known.length === 0) {
  return degradeToVerifiedFieldsOnly(); // never show unverifiable prose
}
```

Prose with zero citations is ungrounded too — it was written from the model's
own memory. Note that the schema deliberately does not ask the model whether it
is grounded: a model that fabricates a fee will happily also claim the answer is
grounded, so the flag would be worthless. Groundedness is computed here, from
the record.

## Never return raw LLM output

Route handlers return a validated, typed DTO. The model's raw text never reaches
the client unexamined.

## Do not stream the explanation

Streaming would show the user prose before citation validation could reject it,
which defeats the entire contract. Generate, validate, then respond.

## Decoding

`temperature` 0–0.2 with a fixed `seed`. Set an explicit `timeout` and
`maxRetries`, and degrade to the verified fields on failure rather than showing
an error where guidance should be.

## Keep the two modes separate

Questions about a government service and general questions are different code
paths with different prompts, and are labeled differently in the UI. Never blend
verified government facts and general world knowledge into one unlabeled answer.
